<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    The callee clause of `option argclobber`: a callee that READS the argument
    register before writing it keeps its argument, whatever the caller's side
    looks like.

    The caller here is byte-for-byte the shape the option is FOR, the same one
    tests/stages/kuna-argclobber.xml drops: one path calls `clobber` (which
    destroys rdx and whose own first act is a call, so `calleepreserves` cannot
    narrow the killed set), the other sets edx itself, the two join at the call.
    The only difference is the callee:

        reader: mov %rdx, %rax ; ret

    The caller-side evidence cannot tell this apart from the phantom, and the
    callee settles it: bytes it reads at entry are a parameter however the
    value got into the register. `calleedeadarg` already decodes each callee
    once per image for the opposite claim (does it overwrite the register
    before reading it?), so this reads the same summary's `proves_input` half.

    This is the clause that answers for a callee kuna never recovered as
    variadic. u-boot -O2 `sub_60873270` is printf, called from 1,924 sites, and
    its prologue spilling r1-r3 into the va_list save area settles every one of
    them at once; without this clause
    `printf("%s %s: Invalid \"bus-width\" value %u!\n",dev,name,width)` at
    0x60828000 loses the value %u prints.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 "active-input-trial-scoring".  Both passes must
    render the same four-argument call, so the assertion counts 2.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000">
534889fb4885db7407e822000000eb05ba070000004889dfbe05000000e82e00
00005bc3cccccccccccccccccccccccce80b000000c3cccccccccccccccccccc
c3cccccccccccccccccccccccccccccc4889d0c3cccccccccccccccccccccccc
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="caller3"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100030" name="clobber"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100040" name="helper"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100050" name="reader"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>option argclobber off</com>
  <com>lo fu caller3</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option argclobber on</com>
  <com>lo fu caller3</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="argclobber-guards #1: the callee reads rdx before writing it, so the trailing argument survives both passes" min="2" max="2">reader\(a[0-9]+,5,v[0-9]+\)</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
